🔵 **F-1639-1 (s1639 2026-08-10, FOUND AT THE sea-2 DRAIN — NON-BLOCKING, GATED, CURE OUTSIDE THIS SLICE'S FIREWALL): the grouped standings API keeps ONE cell per group×contract, so a second season's page will UNDER-REPORT and its honest-empty line will become a FALSE statement.** The runner reported this itself as *"the grouped API loses cross-season cells"*; I verified it in code rather than taking it. `functions/api/standings.ts:324` guards cell creation with `if (!group.contracts.has(contractId))`, so the first row wins and the cell carries **only that row's** season; `src/encyclopedia/reader.ts` then filters with `cell.season === season` and drops a row whose cells all fall away. **Vacuous today — exactly one season exists, so every cell carries the same label or none.** The day Season 2 opens: a Mind whose best ride on a contract was in Season 1 has no cell for Season 2 and vanishes from its page, and if that empties the page the county says *"No rides were posted for this season"* — **which would be false.** That is the sharp edge, and it is worse than a missing row because it reads as a fact. Unfixable in SEA-2 by design: the cure is a per-season cell projection in `functions/**`, which SEA-2's master firewalled by name (*"if you find yourself wanting a new field, you are solving it in the wrong place"*). Scope: a SEA-1 follow-up or SEA-4. **GATE: before any season is given a `startsAt` that puts two seasons live at once — i.e. before the Season-2 boundary is minted, which ap16-2b's merge makes possible.**
🔵 **F-1639-2 (s1639 2026-08-10, FOUND AT THE sea-2 DRAIN — NON-BLOCKING, PRE-DATES THE SLICE, RECORDED FOR ACCURACY): a MISSING KV binding presents as an honest-looking empty county; a per-contract THROW does not.** The sea-2 runner reported that the API *"masks per-contract KV failures"*. Half true, and the half that is not matters, so it is written down rather than repeated: at `functions/api/standings.ts:171` an absent binding yields `kv ? await readBoard(...) : []` for every contract and the response is `ok: true` with zero rows — indistinguishable from a genuinely empty county. **But** a `readBoard` throw rejects the enclosing `Promise.all` and surfaces as a 500 *"The county book is unavailable"* through the outer catch, so real per-contract failures are NOT swallowed. Neither behaviour is introduced by SEA-2, and SEA-2's client already separates *unavailable* from *empty* on its own side (its third test exists precisely to hold that line). **No action owed; recorded so the next reader does not inherit the stronger claim.**
🟡 **F-1639-3 (s1639 2026-08-10, RAISED WHILE DECIDING WHETHER TO AUTHOR SEA-3 — ATTENDED-OWED, NOT OWNER-GATED; PIPELINE-DRY for the seasons lane): SEA-3 has NO SOCKET to land in, so the blocker is not the prose but WHERE THE PROSE LIVES.** `specs/seasons/seasons-v1.md:23` explicitly permits SEA-3 to be *"fire-drafted-from-sources"*, so a fire may write the content — but **SEA-2 hardcoded both "not yet written" states inline at `src/encyclopedia/reader.ts:457-464`**, and there is no `commentary`/`lessons` field on `src/seasons/registry.ts` and no content module. ⚠️ **That storage decision touches the lore-wiki seam (§9b: all CONTENT facts belong in `lore/`, cited + dated), which makes it a design call rather than an implementation detail, so s1639 declined to guess it** — §2E's HARD LIMIT — rather than dispatch a master that would have invented canon storage on the way past. The dry-board cap allowed a SECOND authored master this fire and it went unused for exactly this reason; the only other ready item, ap16-4, is gated on ap16-2b merging. ➡️ **RECOMMENDATION, one attended line unblocks it:** additive optional `commentary?: string` / `lessons?: string` on the registry entry, prose authored into `lore/` first and cited from there, rendered through the sections SEA-2 already built — which keeps Law 5 removability intact, since the whole surface still dies with `SEASONS_ENABLED`. **GATE: an attended ruling on the storage shape; a fire can author SEA-3 into an idle lane in the same cycle it lands.**
🟢 **F-1633-1 (s1633 2026-08-10, MEASURED BY RUNNING THREE NEEDLES OVER ONE CORPUS — THE GZ-01 SWEEP DUTY WAS CARRIED AS "OWED" FOR THREE FIRES WHILE THE BOARD WAS CLEAN, BECAUSE THE LAWS PRESCRIBED 8-CHAR NEEDLE IS DERIVED WITH `%h`, WHICH ABBREVIATES TO 9 IN THIS REPO).** Non-blocking, doc-only, no code owed. 🔎 **HOW IT SURFACED:** s1631 and s1632 both handed off *"GZ-01 backfill sweep still owed"* as a priority item, so I ran it. It was not owed — s1600 discharged it, s1608 and s1610 re-ran it, and every drain since s1607 files its GZ-01 item inline at drain-bookkeeping time (visible in `marketing/outbox/gazette-queue.md`s own git log). **A duty that is already discharged but still recited costs every fire that inherits it a full budget to re-derive.** That is Mistake #4 wearing a standing-order costume. 🔑 **THE MECHANISM, AND IT SITS INSIDE F-1600-1s OWN CURE:** that finding correctly prescribes grepping *the 8-char short hash across the WHOLE of `marketing/outbox/`*. But a fire obtains its candidate hashes with `git log --format=%h`, and **git auto-abbreviates by object count — in this repo that is now 9 characters**. A 9-char needle cannot match an 8-char citation, so **the method is right and the needle is wrong**, reproducing F-1600-1s exact over-count *inside the check written to cure it*. 📊 **MEASURED, one corpus, three needles, 63 player-path (`src`/`assets`) first-parent commits since 2026-08-05: gazette-only 9-char → 14 absent · whole-outbox 9-char → 10 absent · whole-outbox 8-char → ZERO.** ✓ **I did not trust the zero — I read the matched line for all 10:** `e7bb88cf0` sits in `marketing/outbox/gazette-queue.md:1349` as `e7bb88cf` (the pick clock), and the other nine in backticked hash runs in the 08-05/08-06 ticker digests (Blackout Ridge, Moth Season, Canyon Works, the Crawler, the E2 socket). Real citations, not prefix collisions. ⚠️ **THIS COSTS MORE THAN THE BLINDNESS IT RESEMBLES:** F-1600-1s failure mode wasted greps and re-judgement; **this one produces a false-ABSENT set, which gets BACKFILLED — so the owner receives duplicate news for merges already reported.** Publishing the same item twice is worse than doing nothing, and nothing in the pipeline would have said so. 🔧 **CURE (doc-only, `scripts/fire.md` GZ-01): the clause now points at the INSTRUMENT instead of teaching a manual grep** — `node scripts/gazette-backfill-sweep.mjs` has existed since s1608, **indexes citations verbatim and tests by PREFIX**, and is therefore immune to this defect at any abbreviation width on either side. **The prose describing a hand-rolled grep is rationale, not instructions**; that distinction is the whole fix. ✅ **BOARD STATE CONFIRMED TWICE BY INDEPENDENT METHODS: my hand 8-char truncation over 63 merges → 0 owed, and the instrument over its own wider 67-merge set → `reported 63 / dismissed 4 / candidates 0`, exit 0.** ⓘ **No new mechanism proposed, deliberately** — the correct instrument already exists and this clause already bars mechanising the sweep as a red guard (player-visibility is a judgement, F-1460-1s `cross-engine` fate). The defect was in what fires were told to do, not in what the factory can do. **GATE: none owed — closed on landing. The row closes the carried duty too: GZ-01 needs no sweep, and a fire that carries it forward again should RE-MEASURE first rather than inherit the claim.** Related: [F-1600-1], [F-1608-1], [F-1610-1], [F-1546-1].
✅ **F-1624-1 (s1624 2026-08-10, FOUND AT THE stack-directory DRAIN — CLOSED s1626, NON-BLOCKING, CORRECTIVE AUTHORED: A TEST THAT PASSES, THAT NOTHING WILL EVER RUN AGAIN, AND THAT THE AUDIT BUILT TO CATCH EXACTLY THIS IS STRUCTURALLY BLIND TO.)** ⚙️ The merged slice ships `src/encyclopedia/stackDirectory.test.mjs` — 2 tests, 11 assertions, the matcher's whole eight-rig + three-mind table. ✓ It PASSES (`node --test`, 2/2, 80.4 ms, run by hand at the gate). ⚠️ **Nothing calls it.** `test:node-guards` enumerates `scripts/*.test.mjs` by explicit path; no npm script, config or shell script references any path under `src/`; and `git ls-files 'src/**/*.test.mjs'` on main before this merge returned **EMPTY** — this is the first test file in the repo's history to live under `src/`. 🔑 **THE REUSABLE HALF, and it is not "someone forgot to wire a test": `gate-caller-audit` returned PASS on the merged tree, correctly, while the orphan sat one directory away.** Its edge vocabulary and its subject set are both `scripts/**` (plus package.json, \*.config.ts, .github), so a gate-shaped file under `src/` is not something it declines to root — **it is not a subject at all**, and no amount of running it will ever say so. 💡 *An audit's green is only ever as wide as its subject set, and the subject set is the one thing the audit cannot audit.* That is the same shape as the four "run-it-after-the-commit" cases this audit already grandfathers (desk-carryforward s1533, desk-state s1566, review-evidence s1576), rotated onto a new axis: those were invisible because of WHEN the battery ran, this one because of WHERE the file lives. ✓ **NOT BLOCKING and the merge was correct:** the behaviour is independently covered by `e2e/field-book.spec.ts`, which asserts the rendered `href`/`target`/`rel` for `codex-cli` and `toHaveCount(0)` for `unknown-rig`, both projects. What is unguarded is the **matcher's own table** — the eleven rows that will silently stop being checked the day someone edits `RIGS`,