🔬 **F-1611-2 (s1611 2026-08-10, MEASURED ON MY OWN GATE — A COLD EXTERNAL DEV SERVER MANUFACTURES WANDERING REDS THAT IMPERSONATE A LOAD CEILING).** Non-blocking; a note about the INSTRUMENT, and I nearly wrote it up as a defect of the slice. 📊 **THE NUMBERS:** first two runs of the target spec against a freshly-started `GR_CAPTURE_EXTERNAL_SERVER=1` dev server failed **3 of 6**, then **1 of 6** — and the failing MEMBERS MOVED (`:101`/`:216`/`:240` → `:129`), every one dying inside `installAgentTools`'s `page.evaluate`. Each **passed ALONE in 2.9s**; runs 3 and 4 were **6/6 in 15.5s and 15.3s**, i.e. *faster* than the control's own first run (18.2s). ➡️ **The cause is vite's cold transform of the whole `Game` module graph on first request, paid by run 1 and charged to the per-test timeout.** ⚠️ **This is a DIFFERENT AXIS from F-1270-1 and must not be read as a counter-example to it:** `--workers=1` was honoured on every run here, so the serialisation law did its job and this failure is upstream of it. 💡 **The trap is that moving members are the textbook signature of the F-1095-2/F-1084-1 load-sensitive class, so the honest-looking verdict — "known flake, load-sensitive" — is available immediately and is WRONG about the cause**; only re-running after warm-up, and comparing against a control that was warmed the same way, separates "my harness was cold" from "this tree is fragile". ⓘ One `fetch` of `/skill.md` is NOT enough warm-up — it proves custody of the tree (which it is worth doing, and did) but touches almost none of the module graph. **GATE: none owed — a pointer for the next fire that gates against an external server. Discard run 1, or warm with a real page load, before drawing any verdict.** Related: [F-1270-1], [F-1095-2].

