✅ **F-1608-3 (s1608 2026-08-10, HAPPENED AND CURED IN-FIRE — §3.0b's CURE AND THE ART RUNNER'S BROAD `git add` COLLIDE, AND THE COLLISION IS STRUCTURAL, NOT BAD LUCK).** While I gated f1607-1 in a detached worktree exactly as §3.0b orders, the ART runner finished f1608-1 and committed with a broad add. Its commit `6c780b621` swept **`gate-s1608` into main as a GITLINK** — `160000 74bd342431f48e93e62aa4410db5243174521d91 0 gate-s1608`, i.e. a submodule-shaped pointer at my gate worktree's merge commit — along with the artifact/log churn I had deliberately left alone. Its own log records the warning it ignored: *"adding embedded git repository: gate-s1608"*. **Removed this fire** (`git rm --cached`, disk untouched); the worktree was already gone, so the entry pointed at a path that does not exist and would have been garbage in every future clone. 🔑 **WHY THIS IS A STANDING TRAP AND NOT ONE RUNNER MISBEHAVING: TWO HOUSE RULES REQUIRE THE COLLISION.** §3.0b says gate undecided content in a **detached worktree** so your gate cannot leak into main — and the permission gate requires that worktree to live **INSIDE the repo root** (a sibling path is refused). So the very directory created to keep a gate out of main sits **directly in the path of any concurrent broad `git add`**, and the ART runner is the one writer in this factory that still uses one. §3.0b protects the *contents* of a gate and is silent about the *container*. ⚠️ **The failure is SILENT in the direction that matters**: nothing reddens, `git status` looks clean afterwards, and the gitlink is a single innocuous line in a 20-file art commit — it survives review by being boring. ➡️ **MITIGATION, cheap and available today: name the gate worktree so it is ignored** — `.gitignore` already cannot help (it is tracked by explicit add), but `gate-*/` added to `.gitignore` WOULD stop the broad add, since `git add -A` honours ignores. **Recommended, not done this fire:** it is a one-line repo-config change touching every