nd it is green:** `ACTIVE_TILE_ID === 'e1-twin-banks' && moveTarget.x >= minX && moveTarget.x <= maxX ? Math.sign(moveTarget.x - blocker.x) || this.avoidanceSide() : this.blockerSlideDirection('x', moveTarget)` — shipped `5c27a1b5c` (*runner(lane-c): f1441-2-crossings-keep-their-z*, 2026-08-04). 🔑 **Two differences from the failed deadband, both load-bearing: (1)** it is goal-relative to the **BLOCKER'S CENTRE** (`moveTarget.x - blocker.x`), which is *stable* as the enemy moves, where the F-1510-1 defect is enemy-relative (`moveTarget[axis] - position[axis]`) and therefore **flips mid-slide**; **(2)** it gates on **the goal lying inside the blocker's padded span** — the head-on geometry itself — rather than on a magnitude, which is the thing the deadband kept failing to detect. 💡 **The reusable lesson is the embarrassing one: this class of conflict had ALREADY been solved once in this very file by scoping on GEOMETRY, and the factory then spent a fire trying to solve it with a SCALAR. A master proposing a mechanism should be required to say why the existing mechanism for the same problem does not apply.** 📋 **SCOPE:** test whether generalising that branch off `ACTIVE_TILE_ID` (apply it to any blocker whose padded span contains the goal) greens both judges. **FORBIDS** reverting `531bd923a`, editing either e2e spec, and re-pinning `scripts/gr-sim.test.mjs` (F-1441-3); **REQUIRES** `test:node-guards` (F-1460-1 path trigger — the diff touches `src/entities/`) and all four `Terrain.landmarkBlockers()` consumers (F-1510-2, via `grep -rl landmarkBlockers e2e/`). ⚠️ **NOT AUTHORED s1511 by choice, and the reason is the honest half:** choosing that consumer battery honestly is the larger part of the work, and `e1-twin-banks` may depend on tile-specific footprint geometry that does not generalise — a NEGATIVE result must be licensed again. **GATE: closes when one tree has `landmark-collision:68` AND `never-trap:88` green on both projects at `--workers=1`, or a measured negative result shows the centre-relative span gate does not generalise.** Related: [F-1510-1], [F-1511-1], [F-1511-3], [F-1441-2], [F-BW-10]. ✅ **AUTHORED + DISPATCHED s1512 as `tasks/lane-f1511-2-blocker-slide-geometry-gate.md` → lane-c — and the hypothesis was MEASURED GREEN FIRE-SIDE BEFORE the lane run was spent, because s1510 and s1511 had each already spent one on this thread.** Detached worktree `gate-s1512` at base `6d1810dbd`, external vite on scratch port **5199** (`strictPort`), `--workers=1`, both projects. **Isolated + controlled headline: baseline `landmark-collision:68` 2 FAILED (reproduced twice), treated 2 PASSED.** Full pair with `never-trap`: baseline **rc=1, 4 failed / 14 passed**; treated **rc=0, 18/18 passed**, reproduced twice. **`never-trap` 8/8 green on every arm.** `tsc` rc=0; `test:node-guards` rc=0 with **no Baron sim pin moved** (the F-1460-1 hazard, checked rather than assumed). 🔑 **WHY IT WORKS, read from the code:** inside the padded span the approach is head-on, so `Math.sign(moveTarget.x - blocker.x)` is ~0 and the `|| avoidanceSide()` fallback restores the stable go-around **scoped to that geometry**; outside the span `blockerSlideDirection()` still applies, so F-BW-10 stays cured. That is precisely the axis the deadband could not reach, because it asked about a MAGNITUDE. **A negative result is re-licensed in the master anyway** — the fire-side arm is a 2-line patch in a scratch worktree, and a runner disagreement would be worth more than the confirmation. Evidence: `docs/bench/f1512-1-blocker-slide-geometry-gate.md`. Related: [F-1512-1], [F-1512-2].
542:🔬 **F-1507-1 (s1507 — THE LANE/FIRE GUARD DIVERGENCE IS A NODE VERSION, NOT A LOAD FLAKE, AND THE MECHANISM IS A DOTFILE. TWO FIRES DIAGNOSED IT AS TIMING; MEASUREMENT SAYS OTHERWISE.** Cure for the *message* landed `690fa990d`; the *instrument* split is on the desk.) F-1506-1 recorded `scripts/node-guards-timeout.test.mjs` red in the lane shell and green in the fire shell on the same tree, attributed it to concurrent playwright load — **explicitly, and to its credit, marking that attribution UNVERIFIED** — and recommended *"widen the bound or assert the ordering rather than the millisecond."* ⛔ **THAT REC IS WITHDRAWN: there is no ordering to assert, because on the lane's node NEITHER SIBLING EVER RUNS.** 🎯 **THE MECHANISM, END TO END, EVERY LINK MEASURED THIS FIRE.** ⑴ `~/.zshrc:54` runs `nvm use 23` and `~/.nvm/alias/default` is `23`, while the repo's own `.nvmrc` pins **26.4.0**. ⑵ zsh sources `.zshrc` for **interactive** shells only, so the shell FORM decides the node: measured `zsh -ic` → **`~/.nvm/versions/node/v23.11.1/bin/node` v23.11.1**, whereas `zsh -lc`, `zsh -c`, `bash -lc` and this fire all → `/opt/homebrew/bin/node` **v26.4.0**. ⑶ On node **≤ v23.x, `--test-timeout` is applied at FILE granularity**: the fixture is cancelled as one unit and a per-test `{ timeout }` is **never consulted**. Measured with the guard's own fixture (two 3000 ms tests, one bare and one `{ timeout: 10_000 }`, under `--test-timeout=1000`): **v23.3.0 / v23.4.0 / v23.11.1 → `tests 1`, file cancelled whole; v24.14.0 / v26.4.0 → `tests 2`, per-test bound wins.** ⓘ On v23 the flag `--experimental-test-isolation=none` flips it to per-test and `=process` keeps it file-level — but on **v24+ all three isolation settings give per-test**, so isolation is the v23-only lever and **v24+ cannot produce the lane's result under any setting.** ⑷ That reproduces the lane's recorded output **exactly**: `ℹ tests 1 / pass 0 / fail 0 / cancelled 1` with the FILE as the failing entry at ~1002–1005 ms. 🧩 **THE APPARENT CONTRADICTION THAT NEARLY SANK THIS, RESOLVED:** the lane's *outer* run reported `tests 2`, which looks like a v24+ signature and briefly refuted the whole hypothesis. It does not — the outer run passed **no** `--test-timeout`, so nothing was cancelled, and v23 reports subtests normally when nothing dies. **`tests 1` is an artifact of file-level CANCELLATION, not of the version's ordinary reporting.** ✅ **THREE HYPOTHESES REFUTED BY MEASUREMENT BEFORE THE FOURTH SURVIVED, recorded because each looked right:** *node version as read from `zsh -lc`* (returns v26.4.0 — the wrong shell form, and believing it would have cleared the real cause); *cwd* (the guard spawns with `cwd: ROOT`, my first probe used the fixture dir — both arms PASS fire-side); and *the tree* (the guard runs green in **all five** worktrees under `/bin/zsh -lc`, guard blob identical `sha256:ce10c74b540c` in each). ⚠️ **THE CONSEQUENCE THAT OUTLIVES THE GUARD, WHICH IS THE REASON THIS ROW IS NOT COSMETIC.** `scripts/run-node-guards.mjs` passes `--test-timeout=300000`. On the lane's node **that bounds each FILE at 300 s, not each test.** Therefore ⓐ the battery's declared per-test budgets — the escort test's **120 s, which F-1410-2 forbids raising**, and Twin Banks' 45 s — are **not honoured as per-test budgets in the lane at all**; and ⓑ F-1400-4's stated *"~8.8× margin"* (34.2 s slowest **test** vs 300 s) is the wrong denominator there. The right one is the slowest **file**: `scripts/gr-sim.test.mjs` at **55.6 s**, i.e. **5.4×** — and that margin shrinks every time a test is added to that one file, silently, with no guard watching it. 🩺 **LANDED THIS FIRE (`690fa990d`), scope deliberately tiny:** the guard's red now carries a DIAGNOSIS naming the node, the granularity, and the cure, and says in terms that it must **not** be cured by loosening the assertion (F-1441-3). **Proven BOTH ways by executing the violation path on the node that violates it, never by reading a green (s1299/s1300):** v26.4.0 → rc=0, pass=2, **no** DIAGNOSIS emitted; v23.11.1 → rc=1, pass=1 fail=1, DIAGNOSIS emitted and naming `.nvmrc`. Full battery after the edit: **rc=0, 345 tests, 342 pass, 0 fail, 3 skipped, 205.9 s.** 🚫 **WHAT I DID NOT DO, AND WHY:** I did not unify the node. `~/.zshrc` is **outside the repo** — Robin's personal dotfile — and pinning the lane runner to `.nvmrc` instead would change the instrument **every lane-side gate has used**, which is a factory-wide blast radius no fire should trigger unattended (§7.3, §7.7). ⓘ **HONEST LIMIT: I did not observe the Codex sandbox's node directly, so the exact shell form the runner gets is UNVERIFIED.** What is verified is that the lane's behaviour matches v23.x precisely and is **impossible on v24+ under any isolation setting**, and that a `nvm use 23` line exists to produce it. **REC: rule on unifying lane and fire onto `.nvmrc`'s 26.4.0 — the cheapest version is one line in `~/.zshrc` (`nvm use 23` → `nvm use`), which is the owner's file; the in-repo alternative is resolving node from `.nvmrc` in `scripts/lane-runner-v3.sh` (inert until the runner restarts). GATE: closes when a lane-side and a fire-side run of `test:node-guards` are shown to execute on the same node version, measured on both sides, not argued.** Related: [F-1506-1], [F-1400-4], [F-1410-2], [F-1441-3], [F-1269-1], [F-1270-1], [F-1460-1].
