950:🟡 **F-1390-1 (s1390 — THE night3d-perf RATIO GATE NOW FAILS ON AN IMPROVEMENT. ATTENDED CALL, NON-BLOCKING.)** `e2e/night3d-perf.spec.ts:67` ("daylight matrix and Night Shift pressure stay within the painted 115% p95 gate") has been red on main since `992f4066` (2026-08-01), and the predecessor row establishes WHY: the gate compares the 3D terrain path against the **painted 2D path**, and the 2D path got 3–8× faster. **The subject of the gate is inside its own absolute budget** — the very next assertion, `expect(terrain3dP95Ms).toBeLessThanOrEqual(Balance.render.night.frameBudgetMs * Balance.render.night.collapseRatio)`, passed in all six of my runs at ~15–23 ms p95. So the red carries no player-facing meaning today. ⚠️ **But do NOT just raise the number.** The gate was written when painted was the shipping default and 3D was the challenger; "3D costs no more than 115% of painted" was then a real shipping question. Now that 2D is cheap, the honest readings of the same intent diverge: **(a) RE-BASELINE to the absolute budget** — drop the ratio assertion and keep the frame-budget one, on the grounds that what protects the player is ms-per-frame, not a comparison against a renderer being retired; **(b) KEEP the ratio and treat ~1.4–1.6 as a real 3D perf target**, i.e. accept that the 3D path is now the expensive one and book optimisation work; **(c) re-baseline the RATIO to a measured multiple** (say 2.0) so it still catches regressions without asserting parity that no longer holds. ➡️ **Recommendation (c) then (a): keep a ratio gate — it is the only thing that would catch a genuine 3D regression — but re-baseline it against measured current values and state the baseline date in the test.** A bar nobody can meet gets muted, and a muted gate catches nothing. ⓘ **Filed rather than fixed because choosing what a gate should ASSERT is a design act, not a drain act**, and because the number to re-baseline to should be measured on an idle machine (my readings carry a live Codex job in the background). Meanwhile the red is EXPECTED and belongs in the suite inventory — but the standing order forbids hand-editing `logs/suite-red-inventory.md`, so it must land via the proper route.
956:**Owed: a bisect for the main commit that crossed the bar.** Not fire-authorable as a fix (it is a perf regression of unknown origin, and the standing order forbids hand-editing the inventory), but the bisect itself is cheap and mechanical. 💡 The class: *"measured under load"* is a reason to withhold a verdict, not a prediction of which way the verdict will go — s1387 and s1388 both leaned toward "probably the shell", and the shell was innocent.
